# CLAUDE.local.md — personal addendum (sample)

> Claude Code auto-loads `CLAUDE.local.md` alongside `CLAUDE.md`. This file is **gitignored** — put your private brand context, live state, and account routing notes here. Public PII-free playbook stays in `CLAUDE.md`.

## Your brand at a glance

- **Brand name**: <e.g. Acme Devotionals>
- **Brand URL**: <e.g. acme.example.com>
- **Voice**: <1-2 sentence description of caption tone>
- **Cadence**: <e.g. one daily post at noon, two feature posts/week>

## Accounts

| Platform | Handle | Login mode | Notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| Instagram | `@your_handle` | auto via `INSTAGRAM_YOURHANDLE_USERNAME` | |
| LinkedIn | `you@example.com` → personal feed | auto via `LINKEDIN_USERNAME` | Or company page id `<numeric>` |
| X | `@your_handle` | auto via `TWITTER_USERNAME` | If you maintain multiple X accounts, list all of them and which content goes where |
| Pinterest | `@your_handle` | auto via `PINTEREST_USERNAME` | Default board: `<board name>` |
| TikTok | `@your_handle` | manual login only | |

## Account routing (multi-account voices)

If you post from more than one X / IG / LinkedIn identity, document which content goes to which account. Example:

- `@brand_official` — product announcements, daily content, warming
- `@your_personal` — long-form articles, builder/dev cross-posts

Skills like `/x-post` take an explicit `account` arg precisely because the browser session can be on either at any moment. Document the routing here so future you (and future Claude sessions) don't post to the wrong account.

## Live state (most recent)

> Keep this current. Useful for picking up where you left off across Claude sessions. Each entry is "what's live" — not a full history. The committed CLAUDE.md docs the playbook; this file docs the actual state.

- **Instagram** — bio, last post, link in bio
- **X** — last tweet, follower trajectory, any standing graduated-access state
- **Pinterest** — boards in use, link in bio
- **LinkedIn** — last company-page post, last personal-feed post
- **Cron** — installed entries (`crontab -l` mirror)

